# GDB

1. build the hello binary

```bash
    make
```

2. run gdb like so

```bash
    gdb ./bin/hello
```

3. set a break point

```
    break hello.c:9
```

4. run the program within gdb

```
    run
```

### Options

continue / c

next / n

step

print variable

example: print i


### Core Files

See lowlevel's [video](https://youtu.be/3T3ZDquDDVg?si=Uxjj5z0lBX3jAui8) for more information, but here's the short and sweet

To get a core file dump when your program seg faults, check the following settings:

1. make sure that the ulimit is unlimited
    this property sets the upper limit on the number of core dumps that the kernel can make

```bash
    ulimit -c
```

if it isn't unlimited, run this to set it that way

```bash
    ulimit -c unlimited
```

2. check the core dump naming convention

```bash
    cat /proc/sys/kernel/core_pattern
```

my ubuntu system had this value by default


|/usr/share/apport/apport -p%p -s%s -c%c -d%d -P%P -u%u -g%g -- %E


lowlevellearning had it set to "./core"

NOTE: this section is assuming that you're runnning linux

[GDB CheatSheet](https://github.com/reveng007/GDB-Cheat-Sheet)
